The theorem is **true**. Here is the proof.

**Proof.**

Define $Y_i := f(X_i)\,X_i^k$ for $i = 1, 2, \ldots, n$. Since $X_1, X_2, \ldots$ are i.i.d. and $f$ is a fixed (deterministic) measurable function, the sequence $Y_1, Y_2, \ldots$ is also i.i.d.

We wish to apply the Weak Law of Large Numbers (WLLN) to $\{Y_i\}$. The WLLN requires only that $Y_1$ be integrable, i.e., $E|Y_1| < \infty$.

Since $f$ is bounded, there exists a constant $M > 0$ such that $|f(x)| \le M$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$. Therefore

$$
E|Y_1| = E\bigl|f(X_1)\,X_1^k\bigr| \le M \cdot E|X_1|^k.
$$

By hypothesis $E|X_1|^k < \infty$, so $E|Y_1| \le M\,E|X_1|^k < \infty$. Hence $Y_1$ is integrable.

Moreover,

$$
E[Y_1] = E\bigl[f(X_1)\,X_1^k\bigr] = \mu,
$$

which is finite (and well defined) since $Y_1$ is integrable.

Applying the Weak Law of Large Numbers to the i.i.d. integrable sequence $\{Y_i\}$ yields

$$
\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n Y_i \xrightarrow{\mathbb{P}} E[Y_1] = \mu,
$$

that is,

$$
\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n f(X_i)\,X_i^k \xrightarrow{\mathbb{P}} \mu.
$$

This completes the proof. $\blacksquare$

